.TH CCOL_DYNMQ_MSG_COUNT 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_dynmq_msg_count \- number of messages that a dynamic queue holds now
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

size_t ccol_dynmq_msg_count(ccol_dynamic_queue *dq);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function gives the number of messages that the queue holds at the
moment of the call. It reads the count under the lock of the queue, so
the count is a consistent snapshot and not a torn read.

A dynamic queue grows on demand and has no fixed capacity, so only two
things bound this count: the messages that the producers sent, and the
messages that the consumers did not receive yet.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
dq   Dynamic queue to ask
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
On success, the function gives the message count; it gives
ccol_invalid_size if dq is NULL.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The function is O(1).
The function is thread-safe.
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
The value is a snapshot: another thread can send or receive the instant
that this call returns, so do not use the value to decide that a later
receive does not block. Use ccol_dynmq_try_recv_zc() when you want a
receive that does not block.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_dynmq_send_zc (3),
.BR ccol_dynmq_recv_zc (3),
.BR ccol_dynmq_try_recv_zc (3),
.BR ccol_circq_msg_count (3)
